  "briefing": {
    "headline": "The simulation predicts that two home days a week would cut leavers over the next 12 months from about 558 to 366, roughly 190 fewer departures (-34%), with employees clearly positive about the change (average +1.03 on a -2 to +2 scale).",
    "most_at_risk": "No group reacts badly; the biggest gains are among long commuters (-37%) and caregivers (-38%), who get back hours a week and time with children or elderly parents, while short commuters gain least (-30%) and describe it as a 'nice bonus' that won't change whether they stay.",
    "risks": [
      "The gain depends on staff trusting the promise that pay, reviews and promotion are unaffected; if home-day workers start to look worse at review time, the goodwill could reverse quickly.",
      "Several employees already say they would like three days or more flexibility, so the retention boost may fade as two days becomes the new normal and expectations rise.",
      "Many voices say they will keep watching the job market, so this lowers attrition but does not fix pay or other reasons people leave. The forecast also covers only 12 months."
    ],
    "design_changes": [
      "State plainly and in writing that working from home on Wednesdays and Fridays cannot count against anyone in performance reviews or promotion. Then check promotion and rating outcomes after the first cycle to prove it.",
      "Brief managers so they apply the policy consistently and cannot quietly discourage home days. Uneven treatment between teams would erode the benefit.",
      "Run it as a tracked rollout. Compare actual quit rates and output against the 'no change' baseline at 6 and 12 months. Before launch, decide whether to add some flexibility on the three office days, such as flexible start times for long commuters, rather than reacting to later pressure."
    ],
    "confidence": "medium, because every group and every sample voice points the same way and the reasons people give (commute time, family care) are consistent. However, this is a simulation built from only 48 modelled voices, so treat -34% as 'roughly a third' and the small differences between groups (30% to 38%) as indicative rather than reliable."
  },
